spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:6:owner: Product Repository maintainers
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:25:- Changing Stage Commit, Stage Push, or status transition behavior before an ADR is accepted.
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:29:- **Stage Agent**: A configured agent that handles a project status.
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:30:- **Stage Commit**: Commit created after an agent successfully completes a stage.
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:31:- **Stage Push**: Optional non-force push after Stage Commit.
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:32:- **Task Branch Integration**: Bringing completed Task Branch commits into the Loop-Start Branch.
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:40:- **REQ-002**: The decision MUST state whether review is configured per Stage Agent or as a separate command mode.
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:44:- **REQ-006**: The decision MUST define interactions with Stage Commit, Stage Push, Task Branch Integration, and Protected Trunk Branch behavior.
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:56:| Configuration location | Per Stage Agent or separate command mode. |
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:57:| Review timing | Before Stage Commit, after Stage Commit, or before status transition. |
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:77:- **AC-003**: Given Stage Commit and Stage Push are reviewed, When Completion Review timing is defined, Then ordering is explicit.
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:85:- **Test Data Management**: Future tests should use temporary Workspace Repository fixtures and fake review commands.
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:87:- **Coverage Requirements**: Future coverage must include review pass, review fail, retry or attention routing, Runtime State exposure, Stage Commit ordering, and Protected Trunk Branch safety.
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:123:4. Stage Commit runs.
spec/spec-design-independent-stage-completion-review.md:125:6. Status transition or Task Branch Integration proceeds.
docs/adr/0005-per-task-git-worktrees.md:13:Personal Symphony runs each dispatched task in an Agent Worktree under `.symphony/workspaces/`. Each Agent Worktree checks out a per-task Task Branch whose name starts with the configured Task Branch Prefix and is created from the Loop-Start Branch. Existing Task Branches are reused on restart, including tasks that already start in an in-progress project state.
docs/adr/0005-per-task-git-worktrees.md:17:Runtime Settings include a Git Policy for:
docs/adr/0005-per-task-git-worktrees.md:19:- Task Branch Prefix.
docs/adr/0005-per-task-git-worktrees.md:21:- serialized Task Branch Integration with a fast-forward-only final Loop-Start Branch update.
docs/adr/0005-per-task-git-worktrees.md:25:Stage Commit still happens before a task moves to its success status. When a stage commit policy enables Stage Push, Symphony pushes the current Task Branch after the Stage Commit and before any auto-merge attempt.
docs/adr/0005-per-task-git-worktrees.md:27:Completed Task Branches may auto-merge into the Loop-Start Branch only when the Loop-Start Branch is not a configured Protected Trunk Branch. The final Loop-Start Branch update is fast-forward only. When the Loop-Start Branch cannot fast-forward directly to a completed Task Branch, Symphony may merge the current Loop-Start Branch into the Task Branch from the Agent Worktree, then fast-forward the Loop-Start Branch to the updated Task Branch. Symphony does not push the Loop-Start Branch after auto-merge.
docs/adr/0005-per-task-git-worktrees.md:29:The default cleanup policy removes the Agent Worktree after a successful merge and keeps the Task Branch. If configured not to keep the Task Branch, Symphony deletes it after the worktree is removed.
docs/adr/0005-per-task-git-worktrees.md:33:Concurrent task work is isolated by Git instead of by convention. Operators can inspect, push, merge, or discard each Task Branch independently.
docs/adr/0008-github-rate-limit-pause.md:17:This pause applies to candidate issue polling. It does not change Runtime Settings defaults, Stage Commit, Stage Push, Task Branch cleanup, auto-merge, or per-task retry semantics.
docs/adr/0006-browser-local-audio-notifications.md:9:The Web Dashboard receives Runtime State snapshots through the Live Dashboard Connection. Operators need an opt-in browser sound when Symphony needs attention or active work finishes, but audio preferences are local browser behavior and should not change the Runtime Contract.
docs/adr/0006-browser-local-audio-notifications.md:21:Audio Notifications remain browser-local and opt-in. Symphony does not add backend notification events, Runtime Settings, audio assets, or notification history for this behavior.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:6:owner: Product Repository maintainers
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:12:This specification defines a Protected Path Policy for Personal Symphony. The goal is to let a Workspace Repository declare paths that agent work must not modify unless a human explicitly authorizes that scope before dispatch.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:18:This specification applies to Runtime Settings, task dispatch validation, changed-path inspection, Stage Commit, Stage Push, Task Branch Integration, Startup Reconciliation, Manual Task Merge, and Batch Pull Request handoff.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:32:- **Workspace Repository**: The repository where a user runs Personal Symphony.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:33:- **Runtime Settings**: The `settings.json` portion of the Runtime Contract.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:34:- **Runtime Contract**: Repository-owned files inside Runtime Home that define behavior.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:35:- **Protected Path Policy**: A repository-owned rule that identifies Workspace Repository paths agent work must not modify without explicit authorization.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:37:- **Task Branch**: A Git branch created from the Loop-Start Branch for one dispatched task.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:38:- **Stage Commit**: A commit created after an agent successfully completes a configured stage.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:39:- **Stage Push**: A non-force push after a Stage Commit when enabled.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:41:- **Manual Task Merge**: A one-shot operator CLI action that integrates selected completed Agent Worktrees or Task Branches into the current Loop-Start Branch.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:45:- **REQ-001**: The first version MUST store Protected Path Policy in Runtime Settings.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:50:- **REQ-006**: Symphony MUST check protected path changes before creating a Stage Commit.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:51:- **REQ-007**: Unauthorized protected path changes MUST pause the task in Human Attention Status and MUST NOT create a Stage Commit.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:52:- **REQ-008**: When unauthorized protected path changes are detected, Symphony MUST NOT run Stage Push, Task Branch Integration, or Batch Pull Request handoff for that task.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:53:- **REQ-009**: Startup Reconciliation MUST check committed Task Branch work for unauthorized protected path changes before integrating into the Loop-Start Branch.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:54:- **REQ-010**: Manual Task Merge MUST check committed Task Branch work for unauthorized protected path changes before integrating into the Loop-Start Branch.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:61:- **CON-001**: The policy MUST NOT overwrite user-edited Runtime Contract files during Bootstrap.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:63:- **GUD-001**: This Product Repository SHOULD protect package and release-sensitive paths once the policy exists.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:67:### Runtime Settings Shape
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:69:The exact field names may be adjusted during implementation, but Runtime Settings MUST support named protected patterns and a human authorization mechanism.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:106:### Recommended Initial Protected Paths For This Product Repository
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:121:- **AC-001**: Given a policy protecting `bin/symphony.js`, When an agent modifies that file without authorization, Then Symphony pauses the task in Human Attention Status before Stage Commit.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:122:- **AC-002**: Given an issue authorizes `bin/symphony.js`, When an agent modifies that path, Then Symphony allows normal Stage Commit behavior.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:123:- **AC-003**: Given committed Task Branch work modifies a protected path without authorization, When Startup Reconciliation runs, Then Symphony refuses integration and records attention.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:124:- **AC-004**: Given Manual Task Merge targets a Task Branch with unauthorized protected path changes, When the command runs, Then Symphony refuses integration before changing the Loop-Start Branch.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:132:- **Test Data Management**: Temporary Workspace Repository fixtures with Git worktrees, policy settings, and synthetic issues.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:134:- **Coverage Requirements**: Cover pattern matching, authorization parsing, Stage Commit block, Startup Reconciliation block, Manual Task Merge block, and unchanged `.gitignore` behavior.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:140:- Unauthorized protected file modification blocks Stage Commit and keeps the Agent Worktree for inspection.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:141:- Authorized protected file modification proceeds through normal Stage Commit behavior.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:142:- Startup Reconciliation detects protected path changes in committed Task Branch work.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:143:- Manual Task Merge detects protected path changes in committed Task Branch work.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:148:Self-dogfooding requires stronger safeguards than prompt instructions. Routine agent tasks should not accidentally change package entrypoints, release workflows, packaged binary scripts, or other sensitive files. A repository-owned Protected Path Policy makes those safeguards explicit and enforceable before commits and integrations.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:150:Runtime Settings is the preferred first storage location because it keeps policy configuration in the Runtime Contract and avoids introducing a new file format before the matching and authorization semantics are stable.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:165:- **INF-001**: Runtime Home - Stores Runtime Settings and Runtime State.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:169:- **DAT-001**: Changed path list - Derived from Agent Worktree and Task Branch comparisons.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:197:- Generated ignored file under Runtime Home: ignore according to `.gitignore`; Protected Path Policy governs Workspace Repository changes that would otherwise be committed or integrated.
spec/spec-process-protected-path-policy.md:202:- Unauthorized protected path changes block Stage Commit and integration paths.
docs/adr/0004-require-repository-root.md:3:Personal Symphony commands will require the current working directory to be the root of a Workspace Repository instead of searching upward for a Git repository. This prevents accidental bootstrap in the wrong repository or from an ambiguous nested path, at the cost of requiring users to `cd` to the repository root before running `symphony`.
spec/spec-tool-stage-context-command.md:6:owner: Product Repository maintainers
spec/spec-tool-stage-context-command.md:20:This specification covers Runtime Settings, validation, command execution, stdin JSON, temp-file JSON, stdout injection, warnings, and tests.
spec/spec-tool-stage-context-command.md:29:- **Context Command**: A local command configured for a Stage Agent that receives structured context and returns prompt content on stdout.
spec/spec-tool-stage-context-command.md:31:- **Workspace Repository Root**: The root directory where Symphony commands are run.
spec/spec-tool-stage-context-command.md:47:- **CON-001**: Context Command execution MUST NOT allow cwd outside the Workspace Repository root or Agent Worktree.
spec/spec-tool-stage-context-command.md:53:### Runtime Settings Shape
spec/spec-tool-stage-context-command.md:97:- **Test Data Management**: Use temporary shell scripts, temporary Runtime Home paths, and isolated Workspace Repository fixtures.
docs/adr/0003-npm-package-with-packaged-binary.md:3:Personal Symphony will provide the `symphony` command through an npm package whose launcher executes a packaged platform-specific binary when available. Published CLI Package payloads include Linux x64, macOS x64, macOS arm64, and Windows x64 binaries under `vendor/`. This keeps `npm install -g ...` as the user-facing install path without requiring OCaml, dune, or opam in each Workspace Repository; local development may still fall back to the source build tooling.
docs/adr/0014-allowed-loop-start-branch-policy.md:9:Protected Trunk Branches prevent automated Task Branch integration into configured trunks, but they do not stop Symphony from starting orchestration on an unsuitable Loop-Start Branch. A Self-Dogfooding Workspace Repository needs a readiness gate so automated dogfood dispatch starts from a dedicated integration branch instead of from the Product Repository trunk.
docs/adr/0014-allowed-loop-start-branch-policy.md:13:Runtime Settings may define `git.allowedLoopStartBranches` as the Allowed Loop-Start Branch Policy. The first version matches literal local branch names only.
docs/adr/0014-allowed-loop-start-branch-policy.md:15:When `git.allowedLoopStartBranches` is omitted or an empty list, Symphony allows any Loop-Start Branch to preserve existing Runtime Contract behavior and Bootstrap defaults. When the list is non-empty, Symphony compares the current named Loop-Start Branch to the configured set during readiness validation. If the branch is not allowed, or the checkout has no named branch, Symphony reports a Readiness Gap with the current branch state, allowed branch names, and the remediation to switch branches or update Runtime Settings.
docs/adr/0014-allowed-loop-start-branch-policy.md:17:The policy is separate from `protectedTrunkBranches`. Protected Trunk Branches still control automated Task Branch integration. The Allowed Loop-Start Branch Policy controls whether automated orchestration may start at all.
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:1:# ADR 0016: Stage Commit Classification and Commit Skill Load
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:9:Stage Commit currently supports a per-stage commit type and message template. That is enough for a
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:11:labels, stage-specific taxonomy, or an optional skill that guides agents whose work will be committed
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:14:Letting agents create commits manually would conflict with Stage Commit ownership. Parsing free-form
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:15:agent prose as commit metadata would also make commit history depend on non-deterministic output.
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:19:Richer commit metadata should be modeled as Stage Commit Classification in Runtime Settings.
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:21:Stage Commit Classification resolves to deterministic metadata such as type, optional scope, and
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:22:ordered tags. Runtime Settings own the classification taxonomy, stage defaults, label rules, and
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:23:conflict behavior. Existing Stage Commit settings remain backward compatible.
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:25:Issue labels may select classification through explicit Runtime Settings rules. Stage Agent
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:27:to the generated commit summary, but they do not override authoritative classification metadata unless
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:28:a future Runtime Settings contract explicitly enables structured completion metadata.
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:30:A personalized commit skill may be configured as commit-specific Stage Skill Load for stages with
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:31:Stage Commit enabled. Symphony may render that skill into the Agent Prompt with the normal Stage
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:32:Skill Load, but the skill guides work only; it does not create the Stage Commit and does not replace
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:33:Runtime Settings classification.
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:35:Missing, malformed, duplicate, or conflicting classification and commit-skill configuration should
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:40:Runtime Settings remain the source of truth for Stage Commit messages.
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:42:Agents can receive repository-specific commit guidance without taking ownership of Git commit
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:45:Stage Push, Task Branch Integration, auto-merge, Manual Task Merge, and Batch Pull Request behavior
docs/adr/0016-stage-commit-classification-and-commit-skill-load.md:48:The first implementation should avoid changing Runtime Contract defaults unless Human attention
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:2:title: Task Branch Integration
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:6:owner: Product Repository maintainers
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:12:This specification defines safe Task Branch Integration for concurrently completed Task Branches. The goal is to allow unrelated Task Branches that start from the same Loop-Start Branch tip to integrate cleanly when `agent.maxConcurrentAgents` is greater than `1`.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:14:Source issue: [#36 Support safe parallel Task Branch integration](https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/36).
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:18:This specification applies to Stage Commit, Stage Push, Task Branch Integration, Merge Attention Status, Human Attention Status, Startup Reconciliation, Task Cleanup Policy, and Batch Pull Request behavior.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:25:- Force-pushing Task Branches.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:32:- **Loop-Start Branch**: The Workspace Repository branch checked out when orchestration starts.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:33:- **Task Branch**: A Git branch created from the Loop-Start Branch for one dispatched task.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:34:- **Task Branch Integration**: The act of bringing completed Task Branch commits into the Loop-Start Branch.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:37:- **Stage Commit**: A commit created after an agent successfully completes a configured stage.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:38:- **Stage Push**: An optional non-force push after Stage Commit.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:39:- **Merge Attention Status**: The Human Attention Status used when agent work completed but its Task Branch could not be auto-merged.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:42:- **Startup Reconciliation**: Startup recovery that checks completed-stage Agent Worktrees for Task Branch commits not present on the Loop-Start Branch.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:46:- **REQ-001**: Stage Commit MUST run before Task Branch Integration, exactly as current behavior requires.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:47:- **REQ-002**: Stage Push, when enabled, MUST run after Stage Commit and before Task Branch Integration.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:49:- **REQ-004**: If the Loop-Start Branch is a Protected Trunk Branch, Symphony MUST NOT run automated Task Branch Integration.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:50:- **REQ-005**: If the Loop-Start Branch can fast-forward directly to the completed Task Branch, Symphony MUST preserve the current direct fast-forward behavior.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:51:- **REQ-006**: If direct fast-forward is not possible, Symphony MUST attempt to update the completed Task Branch from the current Loop-Start Branch inside the Agent Worktree.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:52:- **REQ-007**: The update operation SHOULD merge the current Loop-Start Branch into the Task Branch instead of rebasing the Task Branch.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:53:- **REQ-008**: If the Task Branch update succeeds, Symphony MUST fast-forward the Loop-Start Branch to the updated Task Branch tip.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:54:- **REQ-009**: If the Task Branch update conflicts or cannot complete cleanly, Symphony MUST move the task to Merge Attention Status or Human Attention Status.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:56:- **REQ-011**: Any integration merge commit MUST be clearly identified as integration-only and MUST NOT include new agent-authored file changes beyond the merge result.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:60:- **REQ-015**: Runtime State MUST record enough diagnostics to explain whether integration fast-forwarded directly, updated the Task Branch first, or paused for attention.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:62:- **CON-002**: Symphony MUST NOT force-push Task Branches as part of integration.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:63:- **CON-003**: Direct merge commits on the Loop-Start Branch SHOULD NOT be used for this strategy.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:73:| Task Branch is already contained | Mark integration as already contained and apply eligible cleanup. |
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:74:| Loop-Start Branch can fast-forward to Task Branch | Fast-forward Loop-Start Branch to Task Branch. |
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:75:| Direct fast-forward fails but merge from Loop-Start Branch into Task Branch succeeds | Fast-forward Loop-Start Branch to updated Task Branch. |
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:76:| Merge from Loop-Start Branch into Task Branch conflicts | Move task to Merge Attention Status or Human Attention Status and keep Agent Worktree. |
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:98:- **AC-001**: Given two unrelated Task Branches created from the same Loop-Start Branch tip, When both complete and `agent.maxConcurrentAgents > 1`, Then both can integrate into the Loop-Start Branch.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:99:- **AC-002**: Given two Task Branches modify the same file incompatibly, When the second integration encounters a conflict, Then only the affected task moves to Merge Attention Status or Human Attention Status.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:100:- **AC-003**: Given Stage Push is enabled, When a task completes, Then Stage Push runs before Task Branch Integration and remains non-force.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:101:- **AC-004**: Given the Loop-Start Branch is a Protected Trunk Branch, When a task completes, Then automated Task Branch Integration is skipped.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:102:- **AC-005**: Given Task Branch Integration succeeds, When Task Cleanup Policy is enabled for worktree cleanup, Then cleanup runs only after integration succeeds.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:110:- **Test Data Management**: Create temporary Workspace Repository fixtures, Loop-Start Branch commits, Agent Worktrees, and Task Branch commits.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:117:- Parallel completion of two Task Branches that touch different files integrates both into the Loop-Start Branch.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:118:- Parallel completion of two Task Branches that conflict on the same file leaves the second task in Human Attention and keeps its Agent Worktree.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:119:- A pushed Task Branch is never force-pushed during integration.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:126:With `agent.maxConcurrentAgents > 1`, unrelated Task Branches can start from the same Loop-Start Branch tip. After the first branch fast-forward merges, the second branch may no longer be a direct fast-forward even when the file changes do not conflict.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:128:Rebasing the Task Branch would require force-pushing if Stage Push already published the branch. Direct merge commits on the Loop-Start Branch would change the current fast-forward integration model. Updating the Task Branch by merging the current Loop-Start Branch into it preserves non-force publication and still allows the Loop-Start Branch to move by fast-forward to the Task Branch tip.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:147:- **DAT-001**: Task Branch commit graph - Determines direct fast-forward and update requirements.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:148:- **DAT-002**: Runtime Settings Git Policy - Defines Protected Trunk Branches and Task Cleanup Policy.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:162:Task Branch 1: A -> B (changes file one)
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:163:Task Branch 2: A -> C (changes file two)
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:165:Integrate Task Branch 1:
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:168:Integrate Task Branch 2:
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:169:Merge current Loop-Start Branch B into Task Branch 2, producing D.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:175:- Task Branch already contains the Loop-Start Branch: direct fast-forward may succeed.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:176:- Task Branch has uncommitted changes in Agent Worktree: pause for attention before integration.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:183:- Two unrelated concurrent Task Branches integrate successfully.
spec/spec-architecture-task-branch-integration.md:185:- No Task Branch integration path force-pushes.
docs/adr/0009-startup-reconciliation.md:9:Personal Symphony can restart with completed-stage Agent Worktrees whose Task Branch commits are not present on the Loop-Start Branch. This can happen after a crash, interrupted cleanup, or a protected-trunk skip. Without startup recovery, committed task work can remain stranded while normal dispatch resumes.
docs/adr/0009-startup-reconciliation.md:13:Startup Reconciliation runs once per process startup after the first tracker fetch and before normal dispatch or agent launch. It considers only issues currently in a configured stage `successStatus` that also have an expected Agent Worktree path. Retained Task Branches without Agent Worktrees are outside the recovery scope.
docs/adr/0009-startup-reconciliation.md:15:Candidates are sorted by numeric issue identifier and then Task Branch name. The Loop-Start Worktree must be clean before any candidate is merged. Each Agent Worktree must be a valid Git worktree, be checked out on the expected Task Branch, reference an existing Task Branch, and have no uncommitted changes.
docs/adr/0009-startup-reconciliation.md:17:Safe committed work uses the same Task Branch Integration path as normal completion. Already-contained Task Branches are treated as reconciled. If the Loop-Start Branch can fast-forward directly to the Task Branch, Startup Reconciliation preserves that direct fast-forward. If direct fast-forward is not possible, Startup Reconciliation may merge the current Loop-Start Branch into the Task Branch from the Agent Worktree, then fast-forward the Loop-Start Branch to the updated Task Branch. Successful and already-contained candidates apply the existing Task Cleanup Policy and do not change tracker status.
docs/adr/0009-startup-reconciliation.md:19:Unsafe candidates move to the configured Merge Attention Status and are recorded in Runtime State issue errors. Protected Trunk Branch targets, wrong branches, missing Task Branches, uncommitted Agent Worktree changes, and conflicted integration are attention cases. Startup Reconciliation does not create Stage Commits, does not resolve conflicts, does not rebase, does not force-push, and does not auto-merge into Protected Trunk Branches.
spec/spec-process-agent-context-retry-output.md:6:owner: Product Repository maintainers
docs/adr/0012-manual-task-merge-protected-trunks.md:11:Operators also need a one-shot Manual Task Merge action for known completed Agent Worktrees. In that flow the operator provides explicit issue identifiers, such as `--merge 20` or `--merge #20`, and expects Symphony to preserve Task Branch naming, clean-worktree checks, fast-forward-only semantics, Task Cleanup Policy, and tracker transitions.
docs/adr/0012-manual-task-merge-protected-trunks.md:15:Personal Symphony allows Manual Task Merge to integrate selected Task Branches into the current Loop-Start Branch even when that branch is a Protected Trunk Branch.
docs/adr/0012-manual-task-merge-protected-trunks.md:21:- accepts issue identifiers only, not raw Task Branch names;
docs/adr/0012-manual-task-merge-protected-trunks.md:28:- does not push Task Branches or the Loop-Start Branch;
docs/adr/0012-manual-task-merge-protected-trunks.md:33:Operators can explicitly integrate selected completed task work while on `main` or another Protected Trunk Branch without leaving Symphony's Runtime Contract.
docs/adr/0012-manual-task-merge-protected-trunks.md:35:Automated flows remain conservative: auto-merge and Startup Reconciliation still do not integrate Task Branch work into Protected Trunk Branches.
