spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:2:title: Stage Commit Classification
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:6:owner: Product Repository maintainers
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:7:tags: [process, runtime-settings, git, stage-commit, issue-24]
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:12:This specification defines Stage Commit Classification for Personal Symphony. The goal is to make Stage Commit type or tag selection deterministic, configurable, and safe when issue labels or stage defaults provide commit metadata.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:14:Source issue: [#24 Support richer commit stage tags and custom skills](https://github.com/MatheusBBarni/symphony-orchestrator/issues/24).
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:18:This specification applies to Runtime Settings parsing, Stage Agent configuration, Stage Commit message rendering, and optional Stage Skill Load behavior in a Workspace Repository.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:20:The intended audience is implementers, reviewers, and future agents working on Stage Commit behavior. This specification assumes the current Stage Commit flow remains: agent work completes, Stage Commit runs when configured, Stage Push runs only after a successful Stage Commit when enabled, and the issue moves to the stage success status only after the handoff succeeds.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:24:- Replacing Stage Commit with a separate global commit subsystem.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:25:- Auto-loading a commit skill for every Workspace Repository.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:26:- Allowing generated agent prose to choose unconstrained commit types.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:30:- **Workspace Repository**: The repository where a user runs Personal Symphony and where runtime configuration and state are created.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:31:- **Runtime Settings**: The `settings.json` portion of the Runtime Contract.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:32:- **Stage Agent**: A configured agent entry that owns one or more project statuses and transition behavior.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:33:- **Stage Commit**: A commit created by Personal Symphony after an agent successfully completes a configured stage.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:34:- **Stage Commit Classification**: Repository-owned metadata used to choose the commit type or tag for a Stage Commit.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:35:- **Stage Push**: An optional non-force push of the current Task Branch after a Stage Commit is created.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:36:- **Stage Skill Load**: The ordered set of skills configured for a Stage Agent.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:41:- **REQ-001**: Runtime Settings MUST support a stage-level Stage Commit Classification policy when `commit.enabled` is `true`.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:42:- **REQ-002**: A Stage Commit Classification policy MUST support a stage default classification.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:43:- **REQ-003**: A Stage Commit Classification policy MUST support label-derived classification mappings from issue labels to commit classifications.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:47:- **REQ-007**: If multiple matching labels resolve to different classifications and `commit.enabled` is `true`, Symphony MUST pause the task in Human Attention Status before creating a Stage Commit.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:48:- **REQ-008**: Stage Commit message rendering MUST use the resolved classification wherever the commit message template references `<type>` or a future classification token.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:50:- **REQ-010**: Workspace Repository skills MUST resolve before Codex Home skills when both locations provide a skill with the same name.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:53:- **CON-001**: Stage Commit Classification MUST NOT replace `<generated_message_max_90char>` with unconstrained agent-authored prose.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:54:- **CON-002**: Stage Push MUST remain non-force and MUST still run only after a successful Stage Commit.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:55:- **GUD-001**: Classification names SHOULD be conventional commit types when the repository uses conventional commits.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:56:- **PAT-001**: Implementers SHOULD keep the existing stage-level `commit.type` field as the compatibility fallback.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:60:### Runtime Settings Shape
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:72:        "skills": ["git-commit"],
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:73:        "commit": {
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:101:| Multiple labels map to different classifications | Pause in Human Attention Status before Stage Commit. |
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:102:| `commit.enabled` is `false` | Do not create a Stage Commit and do not require classification resolution. |
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:106:- **AC-001**: Given a commit-enabled stage with a matching issue label, When Symphony renders the Stage Commit message, Then `<type>` is replaced with the mapped classification.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:107:- **AC-002**: Given a commit-enabled stage without matching labels, When Symphony renders the Stage Commit message, Then `<type>` is replaced with the configured stage default.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:108:- **AC-003**: Given a commit-enabled stage with conflicting mapped labels, When the stage completes, Then Symphony pauses the task in Human Attention Status and does not create a Stage Commit.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:109:- **AC-004**: Given a Stage Agent with configured skills, When Symphony prepares the agent launch, Then Stage Skill Load remains ordered and opt-in.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:110:- **AC-005**: Given `commit.push` is `true`, When the Stage Commit succeeds, Then Stage Push runs after the Stage Commit and remains non-force.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:117:- **Test Data Management**: Use temporary Workspace Repository fixtures and synthetic issues with labels.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:119:- **Coverage Requirements**: Cover parsing, classification resolution, commit message rendering, conflict attention, and Stage Skill Load validation.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:125:- Render commit messages for fallback, label-derived, and conflicting classifications.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:126:- Verify conflicting classification does not create a Stage Commit and records attention clearly.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:132:Stage Commit already provides deterministic stage-level commit creation. The missing capability is a repository-owned way to derive the commit classification from issue metadata without letting each agent improvise commit semantics.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:134:Label-derived classification should be deterministic because issue labels are structured tracker data. Conflicts should pause for Human Attention Status because a misleading Stage Commit type creates bad repository history and can affect release automation.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:136:Stage Skill Load should remain explicit. A Workspace Repository may opt into a personalized commit skill for a specific Stage Agent, but Symphony should not globally load one without repository configuration.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:143:- **EXT-002**: Git - Creates Stage Commits and performs Stage Push.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:151:- **INF-001**: Runtime Home - Stores Runtime Settings and Runtime State.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:155:- **DAT-001**: Issue labels - Classification input from the Workspace Repository issue tracker.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:159:- **PLT-001**: OCaml backend - Parses settings and renders commit messages.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:176:    "reason": "Conflicting Stage Commit Classification values: fix, docs"
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:184:- A label mapping references an empty classification: reject Runtime Settings as invalid.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:185:- A commit-disabled stage has classification settings: parse them if present, but do not require resolution.
spec/spec-process-stage-commit-classification.md:192:- Stage Push behavior remains non-force and ordered after Stage Commit.
docs/adr/0002-runtime-contract-in-symphony.md:1:# Keep the Runtime Contract inside `.symphony/`
docs/adr/0002-runtime-contract-in-symphony.md:3:Personal Symphony will create the Workspace Repository Runtime Contract inside `.symphony/` instead
docs/adr/0002-runtime-contract-in-symphony.md:7:the `.symphony/` Runtime Contract.
spec/spec-data-bounded-context-diagnostics.md:6:owner: Product Repository maintainers
spec/spec-data-bounded-context-diagnostics.md:30:- **Runtime Home**: The `.symphony/` directory in the Workspace Repository.
spec/spec-data-bounded-context-diagnostics.md:31:- **Runtime Contract**: User-editable, version-controlled runtime files.
spec/spec-data-bounded-context-diagnostics.md:38:- **REQ-002**: Diagnostics MUST NOT be written to Runtime Contract files.
spec/spec-data-bounded-context-diagnostics.md:40:- **REQ-004**: Full stdout MUST NOT be persisted unless an explicitly accepted Runtime Settings option enables it.
spec/spec-data-bounded-context-diagnostics.md:77:- **AC-001**: Given context diagnostics are written, When Runtime Contract files are inspected, Then no diagnostics appear there.
spec/spec-data-bounded-context-diagnostics.md:89:- **Coverage Requirements**: Success, timeout, truncation, non-zero exit, disabled full-output persistence, Runtime Contract separation, and secret redaction.
spec/spec-data-bounded-context-diagnostics.md:94:Context generation can fail for local reasons. Bounded diagnostics give maintainers enough data to debug command behavior while preserving the privacy and idempotence boundaries of Runtime Home and Runtime Contract files.
spec/spec-data-bounded-context-diagnostics.md:134:- Runtime Contract files remain unchanged by diagnostics.
docs/adr/0013-self-dogfooding-through-integration-branch.md:3:The Personal Symphony Product Repository can also act as a Workspace Repository for its own work, but allowing automated Task Branch integration directly into `main` would bypass normal Product Repository review and release safeguards. Self-dogfooding runs should start from a dedicated non-trunk Loop-Start Branch, such as `symphony/dogfood`, fast-forward merge completed Task Branches into that branch, and open a Batch Pull Request to `main` only after Orchestration Idle.
docs/adr/0001-implicit-bootstrap.md:3:Personal Symphony will bootstrap a Workspace Repository when the user runs the default `symphony` command and required runtime files are missing, while also exposing `symphony init` for explicit setup. This favors a successful first run over an explicit-only flow, and records that file creation from the default command is intentional rather than accidental.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:1:# ADR 0015: Safe Parallel Task Branch Integration
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:9:When `agent.maxConcurrentAgents` is greater than `1`, multiple Task Branches can start from the same Loop-Start Branch tip. After the first completed Task Branch fast-forwards the Loop-Start Branch, another unrelated Task Branch from the original tip is no longer a direct fast-forward even when its file changes do not conflict.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:11:Treating that non-fast-forward shape as merge attention blocks useful parallel work. Rebasing the Task Branch would require force-pushing if Stage Push already published it. Creating a direct merge commit on the Loop-Start Branch would change the current model where the Loop-Start Branch moves only by fast-forwarding to a Task Branch tip.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:15:Automated Task Branch Integration is serialized and keeps the final Loop-Start Branch update fast-forward only.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:17:Stage Commit still runs before Stage Push. Stage Push, when enabled, remains non-force and runs before Task Branch Integration.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:19:If the Loop-Start Branch is a Protected Trunk Branch, Symphony does not run automated Task Branch Integration.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:21:If the Loop-Start Branch already contains the Task Branch, Symphony treats the task as already integrated and applies eligible Task Cleanup Policy.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:23:If the Loop-Start Branch can fast-forward directly to the completed Task Branch, Symphony preserves the direct fast-forward path.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:25:If direct fast-forward is not possible, Symphony merges the current Loop-Start Branch into the completed Task Branch from the Agent Worktree. When that update succeeds, Symphony fast-forwards the Loop-Start Branch to the updated Task Branch tip. The integration merge commit belongs to the Task Branch and exists only to connect the current Loop-Start Branch history with the completed task work.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:27:If the Task Branch update conflicts or the updated Task Branch still cannot fast-forward the Loop-Start Branch, Symphony moves the task to the Merge Attention Status and keeps the Agent Worktree for inspection.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:29:Startup Reconciliation uses the same Task Branch Integration rules as normal completion. Manual Task Merge keeps its explicit operator-selected fast-forward-only semantics.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:31:Runtime State records Task Branch Integration diagnostics so operators can distinguish already-contained work, direct fast-forward, update-then-fast-forward, and attention outcomes.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:35:Unrelated concurrently completed Task Branches can integrate into the Loop-Start Branch without avoidable merge attention.
docs/adr/0015-safe-parallel-task-branch-integration.md:37:Published Task Branches do not need force-push because integration updates are ordinary merge commits on the Task Branch.
